<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">French invasion of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1812">1812</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Patriotic War of 1812 · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s campaign of 1812, known in Russia as the Patriotic War. The Russian army retreated, fought at Borodino, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> burned after its occupation; Napoleon's retreat in winter destroyed most of his army, a turning point that raised the prestige of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-invasion-of-russia.jpg" alt="French invasion of Russia" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albrecht Adam · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IV_corpo_durante_la_campagna_di_Russia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> began the campaign to force the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> to comply with the continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of the United Kingdom. France had been at war with the major European powers since 1792, and Napoleon had won the War of the Third Coalition, the War of the Fourth Coalition and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fifth-coalition">War of the Fifth Coalition</a>. Russia had learned from earlier defeats at Austerlitz and Friedland. Between 1810 and 1812 it built and rebuilt fortresses, created military intelligence offices and formally established the corps system. In April 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> issued an ultimatum demanding that French troops leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. Napoleon chose war, and called it the Second Polish War to win support from Polish nationalists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The multinational <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a> crossed the Neman River on 24 June 1812. Estimates of its size vary, with figures of 400,000 to 500,000 men commonly given, and only about half the soldiers were French. The Russian armies under Barclay de Tolly and Pyotr Bagration, together about 180,000 to 220,000 men, retreated eastward and used scorched earth tactics. Heat, disease, desertion and supply failures cost the French heavily before they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> in August. Mikhail Kutuzov then became commander-in-chief.</p><p>At the Battle of Borodino on 7 September, more than 250,000 troops fought and at least 70,000 were killed or wounded. The French took the main positions but failed to destroy the Russian army. Kutuzov decided at the council at Fili to abandon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> entered it on 14 September to find it deserted and set ablaze by its military governor Fyodor Rostopchin. He waited five weeks for a peace offer that never came and left on 19 October. After losing the Battle of Maloyaroslavets he had to retrace his earlier route. Snow, frost, hunger, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> and attacks by peasants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> followed. Napoleon left the army at Smorgonie on 5 December and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Estimates of the losses differ. Richard K. Riehn gives 685,000 men entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and fewer than 70,000 known survivors, while Adam Zamoyski estimates that as many as 400,000 of the 550,000 to 600,000 who crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> died, counting prisoners who died in captivity. About 100,000 prisoners were taken, and more than 50,000 died. The campaign is also said to have claimed around a million soldiers and civilians. The saying that General Winter defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> is disputed, since Minard's map shows the French losses were highest in summer and autumn, owing to inadequate logistics and disease. The campaign remains present in Russian culture through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a>'s War and Peace and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky</a>'s 1812 Overture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French invasion of Russia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
